      "text": "#\nTo determine the length of the tunnel, we need to analyze the situation step by step:\n\n1. **Understanding the Train's Speed and Time Passed:**\n   The train travels at a speed of 60 miles per hour. In the given scenario, the tail of the train exits the tunnel 3 minutes after the front of the train enters the tunnel. We first convert the time from minutes to hours since the speed is given in miles per hour:\n   \\[\n   3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\div 60 = 0.05 \\text{ hours}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Distance Covered by the Train During This Time:**\n   The train covers a distance equal to its length \\(L_{\\text{train}}\\) in the given time. The general formula for the distance covered by an object traveling at speed \\(v\\) for time \\(t\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = v \\times t\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values:\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{train}} = 60 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times 0.05 \\text{ hours} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Applying the Same Logic to the Tunnel:**\n   The same logic applies to the tunnel. The entire length of the train (6 miles) would have passed through the tunnel during the same 3 minutes. This means that the length of the tunnel \\(L_{\\text{tunnel}}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{tunnel}} = L_{\\text{train}} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{3}\\) miles.",
