      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the length of the tunnel. We know that the train is 1 mile long, and it exits the tunnel in 3 minutes. We also know that the train is moving at a speed of 60 miles per hour. Let's break this down step-by-step.\n\n1. Determine the distance the train travels in 3 minutes:\n   The train is 60 miles per hour, which means it travels 60 miles in 60 minutes. Therefore, in 3 minutes, the train travels:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   So, in 3 minutes, the train travels 3 miles out of the tunnel.\n\n2. Determine the length of the tunnel:\n   Let the length of the tunnel be \\( L \\) miles. The train is 1 mile long, so in 3 minutes, the front of the train travels 3 miles, the middle of the train travels 1 mile, and the tail of the train travels the remaining 1 mile. However, the key is that the tail of the train exits the tunnel exactly at the same time the front of the train enters the tunnel. This means that the subtrahend (distance traveled by the front of the train minus the tunnel length) plus the sum of \\( L \\), the length of the tunnel, and the 1 mile of the tail of the train should equal 3 miles. Mathematically, we can write this as:\n   \\[\n   3 - L + L + 1 = 3\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the left side, we get:\n   \\[\n   4 = 3\n   \\]\n   This equation is incorrect because we made a mistake in understanding the problem. We should instead say that the tail of the train exits the tunnel after traveling the same distance the front of the train travels, minus the length of the tunnel:\n   \\[\n   3 = L + 1\n   \\]\n   Subtracting 1 from both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   2 = L\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{6}\\) miles.",
