      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the relationship between the train's speed, the time it takes for the train to completely pass through the tunnel, and the length of the tunnel.\n\n1. **Understanding the Movement of the Train:**\n   - The train is moving at 60 miles per hour. \n\n2. **Calculating the Distance Traveled by the Train:**\n   - In 3 minutes, the train travels a certain distance. Since 1 hour is 60 minutes, 3 minutes is one-sixth of an hour. Therefore, the distance the train travels in 3 minutes is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = \\left(\\frac{3 \\text{ minutes}}{60 \\text{ minutes}}\\right) \\times 60 \\text{ miles per hour} = 0.5 \\times 60 \\text{ miles} = 30 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculating the Length of the Tunnel:**\n   - Since the train's length is 1 mile and it takes 3 minutes to travel 30 miles, the train must have used the tunnel to cover the remaining distance, which must be equal to the train's length plus the length of the tunnel. In other words:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance linearly traveled + the length of the tunnel} = \\text{Total distance traveled} = 30 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   - Therefore, the length of the tunnel \\( L \\) plus the length of the train (which is 1 mile) equals 30 miles. We can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   L + 1 = 30\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solving for the Length of the Tunnel:**\n   - Solving for \\( L \\):\n   \\[\n   L = 30 - 1 = 29 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe length of the tunnel is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{29}\n\\]",
